  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 158 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe potential for some more active weather arrives Saturday as a \ntrough pivots across the northern Plains. There is still some \nuncertainty regarding the strength of this system and resulting \nprecipitation potential. While models remain inconsistent in regards \nto development, track, and precip type, confidence is increasing \nthat portions of north central Nebraska will see at least some \nminimal precipitation. At this time, the latest probabilities show \nthe potential of receiving at least 0.01 of QPF to be near 70 \npercent (across the Pine Ridge) and at least 50 percent throughout \nthe remainder of the forecast area. When looking at the potential of \nreceiving 0.10 of QPF, there is still a nearly 50 percent \nprobability across the Pine Ridge. As far as precipitation type \ngoes, current thinking is that temperatures should be warm enough to \nsupport all rain for most of the area. Areas across the Panhandle \nand Pine Ridge could see some rain/snow mix by late morning and even \nchanging over to all snow by the evening. Overall, this system is \nfast-moving and therefore the changeover of rain to snow will depend \nhighly on the speed of the colder air behind the trough versus how \nfast the precipitation exits the area. A slightly faster system \nmovement will keep the most of the precipitation as liquid. However, \nif the system slows down and more cold air can push in, a larger \narea could see some snow. A dry forecast will return by Sunday \nmorning lasting through Monday as upper level ridging builds back \nacross the western US and surface high pressure returns over the \nGreat Plains. \n\nTemperatures will return to near normals in the low to mid 50s for \nthe weekend as cool air advection begins to push southward into \nNebraska over the weekend. With 850 mb temperatures generally in the \n2 to 6 degree range combined with partly to mostly cloudy skies, \nsurface temperatures will hover near normals. Overnight lows, \nhowever, will remain fairly mild in the mid to upper 20s through the \nweekend. These lows are still nearly 10 degrees above normals for \nthis time of year.",
